","The change in entropy (ΔS) for a reaction can be calculated using the following formula:

ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS) for products - Σ(n * ΔS) for products

where:
* n is the stoichiometric coefficient of each species in the balanced balanced equation
* ΔS is the molar entropy of each species

In this case, the reaction is:

2 \text{PbS}(s) + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO}(s) + 2 \text{S}_2(g)

The molar entropies are:
*  ΔS(PbS) = 91.2 J/K mol
*  ΔS(O2) = 205.1 J/K mol
*  ΔS(PbO) = 66.5  S/K mol
*  ΔS(SO2) = 248.2  S/K mol

Now, let calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = (2 * ΔS(PbO) + 2 * ΔS(S2O2)) - (2 * ΔS(PbS) + 3 * ΔS(O2))
ΔS = (2 * 66.5 + 2 * 248.2) - (2 * 91.2 + 3 * 205.1)
ΔS = (133 + 496.4) - (182.4 + 615.3)
ΔS = 529.4 - 787.7
ΔS = -257.3

Therefore, the change in entropy for the reaction is -257.3 J/mol K.

Final answer:
-257.3
